#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# extended_gcd

#% text
# `extended_gcd(a, b)` gives `[g, [s, t]]` with `g = gcd(a, b)` and `g == s*a + t*b`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The coefficients are the Bezout coefficients found by the extended Euclidean algorithm.

#% item
# Arguments are integers; the gcd is non-negative.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
extended_gcd(240, 46)
#% output
#= [2, [-9, 47]]

#% cell
extended_gcd(17, 5)
#% output
#= [1, [-2, 7]]

#% cell
extended_gcd(12, 18)
#% output
#= [6, [-1, 1]]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# An inverse modulo 97, from the Bezout coefficient of 35:

#% cell
mod(extended_gcd(35, 97)[2][1], 97)
#% output
#= 61

#% cell
modular_inverse(35, 97)
#% output
#= 61

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The Bezout identity:

#% cell
r = extended_gcd(1001, 357); [r[1], r[2][1]*1001 + r[2][2]*357]
#% output
#= [7, 7]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `gcd`, `modular_inverse`, `lcm`
